---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { facets, works } from '../../lib/catalog';

const stats = [
  ['Works', works.length],
  ['Eras', facets.eras.length],
  ['Geographies', facets.geography.length],
  ['Materials', facets.materials.length],
];
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="About Atrium.Earth, its data model, source records, accessibility posture, and 3D loading strategy.">
  <section class="page-shell" aria-labelledby="about-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1 id="about-title">A digital museum built around records first.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Atrium.Earth preserves the existing sculpture corpus and gives it a public structure that can stand without JavaScript, heavy models, or invented interpretation.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="standard-title">
    <div class="content-grid">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="standard-title">Editorial standard</h2>
        <p>The museum favors plain English, stable metadata, and concise object pages. When source data is incomplete, the page names the gap instead of filling it with decorative language.</p>
        <p>The canonical route for every work is under <code>/works/</code>. Older maker and region routes are preserved as redirect pages and in the generated migration map.</p>

        <h2>Technical standard</h2>
        <p>The front end is static Astro. Pages render as HTML first. Search, filters, timeline state, and 3D loading are enhancements layered onto usable pages.</p>
        <p>Interactive 3D previews use web-ready GLB assets when available. Raw STL source files are kept out of the browser path.</p>

        <h2>Accessibility posture</h2>
        <p>The site uses semantic landmarks, visible focus states, reduced-motion handling, still-image fallbacks, and object metadata adjacent to the viewer. The 3D control is optional; the record is not.</p>
      </div>

      <aside class="aside-panel">
        <h2>Collection snapshot</h2>
        <div class="stat-grid">
          {stats.map(([label, value]) => (
            <div class="stat-card"><strong>{value}</strong><span>{label}</span></div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
